# Task Conventions

## Task File Format

Task files live at `.codestudio/tasks/<ID>.md`. Each task file has these sections:

### ## What
Acceptance criteria — what to build, scope boundaries, and testable statements.

### ## Plan
Subtask checkboxes ordered by dependency. Each subtask = one commit.

### ## Review
Findings after implementation:
- Does the code match ## What?
- Obvious bugs, missing edge cases, untested paths?
- User input sanitized? Secrets hardcoded? Auth enforced?
- Follows existing project patterns?
- Tests covering acceptance criteria?

### ## Log
Decisions, problems, solutions. Written after task completion for future sessions.

## Commit Format

Atomic commits: `type(scope): description`

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `test`, `refactor`, `docs`, `chore`, `ci`, `style`, `perf`

## Task Commands

```bash
python3 .codestudio/task.py next              # get next task (records baseSha)
python3 .codestudio/task.py verify            # run all gates, capture evidence
python3 .codestudio/task.py done              # mark complete (requires evidence)
python3 .codestudio/task.py done --skip-gates # bypass evidence check
python3 .codestudio/task.py block "reason"    # can't proceed
python3 .codestudio/task.py unblock T-XXX     # try again
python3 .codestudio/task.py add "title"       # new task
python3 .codestudio/task.py defer T-XXX "why" # defer backlog item with reason
python3 .codestudio/task.py rollback T-XXX    # dry run: show what's lost
python3 .codestudio/task.py rollback T-XXX --force  # execute rollback
python3 .codestudio/task.py status            # project summary
python3 .codestudio/task.py list              # list all tasks
```
